So solving for b: b = [c * h * (c - 1)] / [k(k - 1)]

Then, rearranged, that gives b / h = [c(c - 1)] / [k(k - 1)]

Which is exactly the equation we needed to prove. So that works! But wait, but hold on, is this all valid? Did I correctly assume that this is a BIBD?

The problem states that each teacher teaches exactly k students, so each block (teacher's class) has size k. Also, any two students are in exactly h blocks (teachers). So yes, this is exactly a (v, k, λ) BIBD where v = c (number of students), block size k, and λ = h. Wait, but actually in BIBD terminology, the parameters are usually written as (v, b, r, k, λ), where v is the number of elements, b the number of blocks, each block has size k, each element is in r blocks, and any two elements are in λ blocks. So in this case, yes, that's exactly our case with v = c, so the formula applies.

